"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[9646],{9646:function(e){e.exports=JSON.parse('{"testId":"biochemistry-test-1","slug":"biochemistry","subject":"Biochemistry","testNo":1,"durationMin":40,"questions":[{"n":1,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 32-year-old man is found to have right-sided colon cancer. His father had similar colon cancer and died at the age of 40. His paternal aunt suffers from endometrial cancer and his paternal uncle also suffers from urothelial cancer. There is no history of familial adenomatous polyposis. Abnormality in which of the following DNA repair mechanisms is responsible for this patient\'s condition?","options":["Nucleotide excision repair","Homologous recombination","Base excision repair","Mismatch repair"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Mismatch repair\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of Lynch syndrome or\xa0hereditary nonpolyposis colorectal cancer (HNPCC) and mismatch repair is\xa0the defective DNA repair mechanism here.\\n\\nHNPCC is an autosomal dominant genetic condition that is associated with a high risk of colon cancer\xa0as well as other cancers including endometrial cancer (second most common), cancers of the stomach, ovary, ureters, etc. It is the most common cause of the syndromic form of colon cancer. Colon cancers are seen at younger ages and usually on the right side.\\n\\nDNA mismatch repair proteins\xa0function as spell checkers\xa0during DNA replication by correcting the\xa0incorrect pairing.\xa0In HNPCC, the proofreading function of the mismatch repair systems (majorly\xa0MLH1\xa0and\xa0MSH2)\xa0is lost leading to the accumulation of errors throughout the genome.\xa0The genomes of these patients show microsatellite instability,\xa0which is characterized by changes in the length of short repeats.\\n\\nDamaging agents\\nDamage caused\\nRepair mechanisms\\n\\nIonizing radiation\\n\\nX-rays\\n\\nAnti-tumor drugs\\n\\nDouble-strand breaks\\n\\nSingle-strand breaks\\n\\nIntrastrand crosslinks\\n\\nInterstrand crosslinks\\n\\nNon-homologous end joining (NHEJ)\\n\\nUV light\\n\\nChemicals\\n\\nBulky adducts\\n\\nPyrimidine dimers\\n\\nNucleotide excision repair (NER)\\n\\nOxygen radicals\\n\\nHydrolysis\\n\\nAlkylating agents\\n\\nAbasic sites single-strand breaks\\n\\n8-oxoguanine lesions\\n\\nBase excision repair (BER)\\n\\nReplication errors\\n\\nBases mismatch\\n\\nInsertions\\n\\nDeletions\\n\\nMismatch repair (MMR)\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Defects in the\xa0nucleotide excision repair\xa0pathway are seen in patients with\xa0xeroderma pigmentosum.\\n\\nOption B: Defects in\xa0non-homologous end-joining are seen in patients with subacute combined immunodeficiency syndrome\xa0(SCID).\\n\\nOption C: Defects in DNA base excision repair are seen in patients with MUTYH-associated polyposis (MAP) syndromes.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":2,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following phospholipids is found only in the mitochondria and helps in the mitochondrial function ?","options":["Lecithin","Plasmalogen","Cephalin","Cardiolipin"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Cardiolipin\\n\\nCardiolipin\xa0is the\xa0phospholipid found only in the mitochondria.\\n\\nIt is an important component of the\xa0inner mitochondrial membrane, where it is essential for the following:\\n\\n• Optimal function of numerous mitochondrial enzymes that are involved in mitochondrial energy metabolism.\\n• Maintenance of the structure of the mitochondrial membrane.\\n\\nClinical correlate:\\n\\nDecreased cardiolipin or alteration in its metabolism results in\xa0mitochondrial dysfunction\xa0seen in Barth syndrome (cardioskeletal\xa0myopathy) which is characterized by an enlarged and weakened heart (dilated cardiomyopathy ), weakness in muscles used for movement (skeletal myopathy), recurrent infections due to small numbers of white blood cells (neutropenia), and short stature.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":3,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 22-year-old woman presented with a 2-week history of progressive severe headache and blurry vision. Clinical examination was significant for bilateral papilledema. A lumbar puncture revealed elevated opening pressure with normal CSF composition. An MRI showed no space-occupying lesions. On further questioning, she admits to taking high-dose multivitamin supplements. Which vitamin excess is likely to cause the above scenario?","options":["Vitamin A","Vitamin E","Vitamin K","Vitamin D"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Vitamin A\\n\\nThe clinical scenario of increased intracranial pressure in the absence of space-occupying lesion points towards the diagnosis of pseudotumor cerebri. Vitamin A excess is the likely cause.\\n\\nClinical features of hypervitaminosis A:\\n\\n• CNS:\xa0Symptoms of raised intracranial pressure (pseudotumor cerebri) such as headache, nausea, vomiting, blurring of vision, ataxia, anorexia\\n• Liver: Hepatomegaly and hyperlipidemia\\n• Musculoskeletal: Thickening of the long bones (bony exostosis), hypercalcemia, soft tissue calcification\\n• Dermatological: Dryness, exfoliative dermatitis, alopecia\\n• Teratogenesis: Pregnant women should not ingest excessive quantities as it can cause congenital malformations in the developing fetus.\xa0Isotretinoin(13-cis retinoic acid) is a drug that is teratogenic and absolutely contraindicated in pregnancy.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":4,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A patient died due to poisoning with a substance that inhibits the complex of the electron transport chain that is marked in the image below. Which of the following is likely to be the substance?","options":["Oligomycin","Carbon monoxide","Antimycin A","Rotenone"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Carbon monoxide\\n\\nIn the given image, the complex of the electron transport chain shown is complex IV\xa0and carbon monoxide inhibits complex IV.\\n\\nMolecules like glucose undergo a series of oxidation reactions to produce CO2 and water. The metabolic intermediates of these reactions donate electrons to NAD and FAD to form NADH and FADH2. They, in turn, donate a pair of electrons to a specialized set of electron carriers called the electron transport chain.\\n\\nThe electron chain is present in the inner mitochondrial membrane. It has are 4 protein complexes, named complex-I, II, III, and IV. These complexes are connected by two carriers- co-enzyme Q and cytochrome C. The complexes and their inhibitors are summarized below.\\n\\nComplexes of ETC\\nInhibitors\\n\\nComplex I: NADH-Q oxidoreductase\\nPiericidin, amobarbital, rotenone\\n\\nComplex II: Succinate-Q reductase\\nCarboxin, malonate\\n\\nComplex III: Q- cyt c oxidoreductase\\nBAL(British anti-lewisite), antimycin A\\n\\nComplex IV:\xa0 cytochrome c oxidase\\nHydrogen sulfide, carbon monoxide, cyanide\\n\\nATP synthase\\nOligomycin\\n\\nThe following image shows different compounds inhibiting the different components in an electron transport chain.","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/8e791938435863a74f5f.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/2d68375cddd7ddfa917f.jpg"}]},{"n":5,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Match the type of collagen with its associated disease entity.\\n\\nTypes of collagen\\nCollagen defect disorders\\n\\n1. Type I\\n\\nA. Stickler syndrome\\n\\n2. Type II\\nB. Alport syndrome\\n\\n3. Type III\\nC. Ehler Danlos syndrome (vascular subtype)\\n\\n4. Type IV\\nD. Osteogenesis imperfecta","options":["1-C, 2-B, 3-D, 4-A","1-D, 2-A, 3-B, 4-C","1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B","1-C, 2-B, 3-A, 4-D"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B\\n\\nThe correctly matched\xa0type of collagen with its associated disease entity is\xa01-D, 2-A, 3-C, 4-B.\\n\\nCollagen is the most abundant protein and approximately 28 types have been isolated. The structure of collagen is a triple helix composed of 3 polypeptide chains having glycine residues at every third position.\\n\\nGenetic defects in the various types of collagen found predominantly at specific sites lead to various diseases summarized in the table below.\\n\\nType of Collagen\\nDistribution\\nDefect\\n\\nType I\\n\\nNoncartilaginous connective tissues, including bone, tendon, skin\\n\\nOsteogenesis imperfecta\\n\\nType II\\n\\nCartilage, vitreous humor\\n\\nStickler syndrome\\n\\nType III\\n\\nExtensible connective tissues, including skin, lung, and vascular system\\n\\nEhler Danos syndrome (Vascular sub-type)\\n\\nType IV\\n\\nBasement membranes\\n\\nAlport syndrome\\n\\nType VII\\n\\nDermal-epidermal junction\\n\\nEpidermolysis bullosa aquisita\\n\\nIn osteogenesis imperfecta, there is a severe decrease in bone mass that makes bones brittle, associated with blue sclerae, dental abnormalities, progressive hearing loss, and positive family history.\\n\\nStickler syndrome is characterized by skeletal changes, orofacial abnormalities, and auditory abnormalities.\\n\\nIn Ehler Danlos syndrome ( vascular subtype ) there are features of classic EDS ( skin and joint involvement ) as well as severe muscular hypotonia after birth, progressive kyphoscoliosis, osteopenia, occasional rupture of the eye globe, and great arteries.\\n\\nAlport syndrome is characterized by the presence of a genetically abnormal basement membrane giving rise to proteinuria and hematuria.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":6,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following has the highest glycemic index?","options":["Glucose","Sucrose","Sugar alcohols","Fructose"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Glucose\\n\\nThe Glycemic index is highest for glucose.\\n\\nThe increase in blood glucose after a test dose of carbohydrate compared with that after an equivalent amount of glucose (as glucose or from a reference starchy food) is known as the glycemic index.\\n\\nGlycemic Index:\\n\\n• Glucose and galactose: 1\\n• Dietary fibers: 0\\n\\nLow glycemic index:\\n\\n• Fructose\\n• Sucrose\\n• Starch\\n• Sugar alcohol","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":7,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 60-year-old patient presented to the casualty in an unconscious state. His blood sugars were high, the ABG(Arterial blood gas) analysis showed metabolic acidosis, and urine tests were positive for ketone bodies. The ketone bodies were not utilized in the liver because it lacks the enzyme:","options":["Thiophorase","Thiolase","Ketoacyl reductase","β hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Thiophorase\\n\\nKetone bodies are not utilized in the liver because it lacks the enzyme thiophorase.\\n\\nThe above clinical scenario points to the diagnosis of diabetic ketoacidosis characterized by excess production of ketone bodies. The liver cannot utilize the ketone bodies (acetoacetate, β-hydroxybutyrate, and acetone) because it lacks the enzyme thiophorase (also called succinyl CoA acetoacetyl CoA transferase).\\n\\nKetone bodies are synthesized in the liver mitochondria (by ketogenesis) and transported in the blood to the peripheral tissues. Here they are utilized by the following steps:\\n\\n1. β-hydroxybutyrate (the most predominant ketone body) is oxidized to acetoacetate by β-hydroxybutyrate dehydrogenase producing NADH.\\n2. Acetoacetate is then activated to acetoacetyl CoA by transferring a CoA molecule from succinyl CoA by succinyl CoA-acetoacetyl CoA transferase (thiophorase).\\n3. Acetoacetyl CoA is cleaved into two acetyl CoA molecules by thiolase and oxidized into CO2 by the TCA cycle.\\n\\nWhen higher than normal ketone bodies are present in the blood or urine, it is called ketonemia or ketonuria, respectively. The overall condition is called ketosis. The metabolism of free fatty acids to produce ketone bodies is exaggerated in pathologic states such as starvation and diabetes mellitus, thus causing ketosis. The continued buffering and excretion of ketone bodies deplete the alkali reserve, causing ketoacidosis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":8,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A patient comes to you with vitamin D deficiency. You advise the patient to take a walk under sufficient sunlight everyday. What is the correct sequence of products formed during vitamin D synthesis?\\n\\n1. 7-dehydrocholesterol\\n2. Cholecalciferol\\n3. 25-hydroxycholecalciferol\\n4. 1,25-dihydroxycholecalciferol","options":["1-3-2-4","4-3-2-1","1-2-3-4","1-4-3-2"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1-2-3-4\\n\\nThe correct sequence of products formed during vitamin D synthesis is 1-2-3-4.\\n\\nVitamin D is obtained by synthesis in the skin with adequate sunlight exposure or dietary supplementation. It helps in regulating calcium absorption and homeostasis. Deficiency results in rickets in children and osteomalacia in adults and is a common problem in areas with inadequate sunlight.\\n\\nThe sequence of vitamin D synthesis is:\\n\\n1. Skin:\xa07-dehydrocholesterol\xa0is nonenzymatically converted to previtamin D on exposure to ultraviolet light. It is converted to\xa0cholecalciferol, which then enters the bloodstream.\\n2. Liver:\xa0Cholecalciferol is hydroxylated to form\xa025-hydroxyvitamin D\xa0(calcidiol), which is bound to a vitamin D-binding globulin and released into the bloodstream. This is the main storage form of vitamin D.\\n3. Kidney:\xa025-hydroxyvitamin D\xa0is further hydroxylated to form\xa01,25 dihydroxyvitamin D (calcitriol). This is the biologically active form of vitamin D. In some cases, it can also undergo 24-hydroxylation to form an inactive metabolite called 24,25-dihydroxyvitamin D.\\n\\nThe steps of vitamin D synthesis are summarized below.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9fcd10e564891b92fc8a.jpg"}]},{"n":9,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A middle-aged man who is a chronic alcoholic presented to the casualty with confusion, ataxia, and diplopia. What is the protocol to treat this patient?","options":["Glucose infusion followed thiamine injection","Glucose infusion alone","Vitamin B1 injection followed by glucose infusion","Thiamine infusion alone"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Vitamin B1 injection followed by glucose infusion\\n\\nThe above clinical history is suggestive of Wernicke\'s\xa0encephalopathy.\xa0The current standard of treatment for patients with symptoms of Wernicke\'s encephalopathy is to give\xa0thiamine 100 mg intravenously before administering glucose-containing\xa0IV fluids.\\n\\nThiamine deficiency\xa0leads to the following:\\n\\n1. Wernicke’s encephalopathy:\xa0It is more commonly seen in alcoholics with chronic thiamine deficiency. It is characterized by:\\n\\n• Global confusion\\n• Truncal ataxia\\n• Ophthalmoplegia\\n• Horizontal nystagmus\\n\\n2. Korsakoff psychosis:\xa0symptoms in addition to those of Wernicke\'s encephalopathy include:\\n\\n• Memory loss\\n• Confabulation\\n\\n3. Beriberi:\xa0It can present with:\\n\\n• Dry beriberi: sensorimotor peripheral neuropathy, muscle weakness\\n• Wet beriberi: tachycardia, cardiomegaly, high-output cardiac failure","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":10,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following lipid metabolic pathways is correctly matched with their rate-limiting enzyme?","options":["Cholesterol synthesis - HMG CoA synthase","Ketone body synthesis - cytosolic HMG CoA synthase","Bile acid synthesis - 7α hydroxylase","Fatty acid β oxidation - Acetyl CoA carboxylase"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Bile acid synthesis - 7α hydroxylase\\n\\nThe correctly matched lipid metabolic pathway with its rate-limiting enzyme is bile-acid synthesis - 7α hydroxylase. It converts cholesterol into 7α hydroxycholesterol.\\n\\nThe activity of the enzyme is regulated by negative feedback mainly via the nuclear bile acid–binding receptor, farnesoid X receptor (FXR). When the size of the bile acid pool(mainly chenodeoxycholic acid) in the enterohepatic circulation increases, FXR is activated and transcription of 7α hydroxylase is suppressed. Whereas, 7α hydroxylase activity is enhanced by endogenous and dietary cholesterol.\\n\\nThe rate-limiting enzyme in cholesterol synthesis is HMG CoA reductase. Cytosolic HMG CoA synthase is involved in cholesterol synthesis whereas mitochondrial HMG CoA synthase is the rate-limiting enzyme of ketone body synthesis. (Options A and B)\\n\\nThe fatty acid oxidation in our body predominantly occurs by β oxidation and the rate-limiting step is carnitine palmitoyl-transferase-I (CPT-I) present in the outer mitochondrial membrane. It helps transport an activated fatty acid (Acyl CoA) inside the mitochondria from the cytosol by binding to carnitine. (Option D)\\n\\nAcetyl CoA carboxylase is the rate-limiting step of fatty acid synthesis.","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":11,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 6-month-old child was brought to the outpatient clinic with features of hypotonia, irritability, and exaggerated startle response. Development milestones were delayed. Fundoscopic examination revealed the finding as shown in the image below. Which of the following enzymes is deficient in this condition?","options":["α-Galactosidase","Arylsulfatase A","β-Hexosaminidase A","β-Glucosidase"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. β-Hexosaminidase A\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of an infant presenting with hypotonia, irritability, exaggerated startle response, delayed milestones, and the image showing the cherry-red spot point to the diagnosis of Tay-Sachs disease. The enzyme deficient in this condition is β-hexosaminidase A.\\n\\nTay-Sachs disease is a lipid storage disorder due to the\xa0deficiency\xa0of\xa0hexosaminidase A leading to the accumulation of GM2 gangliosides. It is\xa0classified into infantile, juvenile, and adult-onset forms based on the age at onset and clinical features.\\n\\nAffected infants usually develop normally until 4-5 months of age. Clinical features of Tay-Sachs disease include:\\n\\n• Muscle weakness (hypotonia)\\n• Seizures\\n• Exaggerated startle response\\n• Macrocephaly\\n• Mental retardation\\n• Macular\xa0cherry-red spots\\n• Blindness\\n• Developmental delay\\n\\nThe deficiency of β-hexosaminidase A and B causes Sandhoff\'s disease. The clinical manifestations of Sandhoff disease are similar to those of Tay-Sachs disease. Infants with Sandhoff disease have hepatosplenomegaly, cardiac involvement, and mild bony abnormalities.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Fabry disease\xa0is an\xa0X-linked\xa0lysosomal storage disorder\xa0caused due to deficiency of\xa0α-Galactosidase.\xa0This leads to the accumulation of\xa0globotriaosylceramide. Clinical features include angiokeratomas, hypohidrosis, corneal and lenticular opacities, and painful acroparesthesias.\xa0Angiokeratomas\xa0are dark red to blue-black, flat or slightly raised, punctate lesions, usually seen between the umbilicus and the knees (called the bathing suit area) and the trunk.\\n\\nOption B:\xa0Metachromatic leukodystrophy\xa0is a\xa0lysosomal storage disease\xa0caused due to deficiency of the enzyme\xa0arylsulfatase A. It leads to demyelination and the patient has a neurodegenerative course.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Gaucher disease\xa0is an\xa0autosomal recessive\xa0lysosomal storage disorder with a deficiency of\xa0acid-β-glucosidase.\xa0It leads to the accumulation of\xa0glucosylceramide.\xa0The features of this disease include growth retardation,\xa0hepatosplenomegaly,\xa0thrombocytopenia, anemia, and\xa0Erlenmeyer flask deformity\xa0of the distal femur. Histology shows the presence of\xa0Gaucher cells with\xa0a characteristic wrinkled-paper appearance. Mental retardation is seen in infants.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/6aba028c7d2652468955.jpg"]},{"n":12,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 20-year-old bodybuilder presented with fatigue on moderate exercise. He had recently started eating raw eggs for protein. Which of the following enzymes is likely deficient in him?","options":["Pyruvate carboxylase","Phosphoenolpyruvate carboxykinase","Glucose-6-phosphatase","Glycogen phosphorylase"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Pyruvate carboxylase\\n\\nThe patient developed pyruvate carboxylase enzyme deficiency.\\n\\nIndividuals who eat an excess of uncooked egg-white, develop biotin deficiency and as a result deficiency of the carboxylase enzymes. This is because a protein called, avidin present in the egg-white binds to biotin in the diet, and makes biotin unavailable for intestinal absorption.\\n\\nBiotin deficiency can cause:\\n\\n• Dermatitis\\n• Mental status changes (depression, hallucinations)\\n• Paraesthesia\\n• Alopecia\\n\\nBiotin serves as a co-enzyme for the synthesis of several carboxylase enzymes such as:\\n\\n• Acetyl-CoA carboxylase\\n• Pyruvate carboxylase\\n• Propionyl CoA carboxylase\\n• Methylcrotonyl-CoA carboxylase","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":13,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 5-year-old female child with polyneuropathy, sensorineural deafness, and cerebellar ataxia has the following finding on fundus examination. Lab reveals increased blood levels of phytanic acid. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Refsum\'s disease","Usher syndrome","Zellweger syndrome","Adrenoleukodystrophy"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Refsum\'s disease\\n\\nThe image shows fundoscopy findings of retinitis pigmentosa. This along with the given clinical scenario is suggestive of Refsum’s disease.\\n\\nRefsum\'s disease is a rare autosomal recessive disorder that results from the defect in alpha oxidation of fatty acids such as phytanic acid in the peroxisome leading to elevated levels of phytanic acid in the blood.\\n\\nIn this condition, the enzyme phytanoyl CoA hydroxylase is defective and causes the accumulation of phytanic acid. Clinically, patients present with dysmorphic facies and\xa0severe neurological symptoms such as\xa0polyneuropathy, retinitis pigmentosa, nerve deafness, ichthyosis, and cerebellar ataxia. Regression of symptoms is observed with restricted dietary intake of phytanic acid. Therefore, management includes avoiding sources of phytanic acid such as dairy products, meat, nuts, and green leafy vegetables.\\n\\nNote: The Zellwager spectrum is a continuum of diseases of persoxisome defects with Zellweger syndrome (most severe) at one end, neonatal adrenoleukodystrophy in the middle (intermediate severity) and infantile Refsum\'s disease (least severe) at the other end.\\n\\nOption B-\xa0Usher syndrome\xa0is characterized by sensorineural deafness and retinitis pigmentosa (but symptoms are not affected by restricting phytanic acid)\\n\\nOption C-\xa0Zellweger Syndrome is also known as cerebrohepatorenal syndrome. It is caused by mutations in the\xa0PEX/Peroxin\xa0family of genes involved in peroxisome biogenesis /peroxisomal enzymes.\xa0There is severe neurologic symptoms and patients usually die in the 1st year of life.\\n\\nOption D-\xa0Adrenoleukodystrophy\xa0is a\xa0disorder of β-oxidation of fatty acids. There is a very large chain fatty acids buildup in the adrenal glands, the white matter of the brain, and testes.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/16ac753b501d8f56f52d.jpg"]},{"n":14,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"While conducting a health camp, you host an awareness campaign on healthy dietary practices. Which of the following substances would you say has the highest respiratory quotient immediately after consumption?","options":["Mixed diet","Ghee","Wine","Meat"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Mixed diet\\n\\nThe food item with the highest respiratory quotient (RQ) immediately after consumption\xa0is a\xa0mixed diet.\\n\\nThis is because, immediately after a mixed meal (containing carbohydrates, protein, and fat), carbohydrates is the most commonly metabolized component, so the RQ reaches 1. But, a few hours after, the body would have used up the readily available carbohydrates, and the RQ reaches 0.70, due to fat metabolism.\\n\\nThe respiratory quotient is the\xa0ratio\xa0of\xa0carbon dioxide produced/oxygen consumed.\xa0The\xa0respiratory quotient\xa0for various substances:\\n\\nSubstance\\nRQ\\n\\nCarbohydrate\\n1.00\\n\\nProtein (option D)\\n0.81\\n\\nFat (option B)\\n0.71\\n\\nAlcohol (option C)\\n0.66\\n\\nIn diabetes, the rate of utilization of carbohydrates reduces in relation to fats, hence RQ falls (approximately 0.7). The RQ is lowest when ketosis is very active.\\n\\nNote: For all metabolic fuels, the energy produced is the same (20 kJ/L of oxygen).","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":15,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following statements are true regarding the regulation of cholesterol synthesis?\\n\\n1. Insulin and thyroxine decrease HMG CoA reductase activity.\\n2. Cortisol and glucagon decrease HMG CoA reductase activity.\\n3. Statins inhibit HMG CoA reductase.\\n4. Cholesterol increases HMG CoA reductase activity.\\n5. Cholesterol represses transcription of HMG CoA reductase mRNA.","options":["1, 2, and 4","2, 3, and 5","2, 3, and 4","1, 3, and 5"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 2, 3, and 5\\n\\nStatements 2, 3, and 5 are true regarding the regulation of cholesterol synthesis.\\n\\nHMG CoA reductase is the principal enzyme involved in the regulation of cholesterol synthesis.\xa0Cholesterol is synthesized from acetyl-CoA mainly in the liver and intestine. This process occurs in the endoplasmic reticulum and cytosol.\\n\\nHMG-CoA reductase reduces HMG-CoA\xa0to mevalonate by NADPH in the pathway of cholesterol synthesis.\xa0This principal regulatory step is targeted by statins, which are drugs that efficiently lower cholesterol by inhibiting HMG-CoA reductase.\\n\\nActivity of HMG-CoA reductase is influenced by the following factors:\\n\\n• By-products of the cholesterol synthesis pathway:\xa0Mevalonate, the immediate product of the reaction, and\xa0cholesterol,\xa0the main product of the pathway, inhibit enzyme activity. These metabolites also repress transcription of HMG-CoA reductase mRNA by inhibiting sterol regulatory element-bidning protein (SREBP) transcription factor.\\n• Hormones: Insulin\xa0and thyroid hormones increase\xa0enzyme activity, whereas glucagon or glucocorticoids decrease it.\\n• Posttranslational modifications:\xa0Phosphorylation–dephosphorylation reactions affect enzyme activity.\xa0AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK) phosphorylates and inactivates the enzyme.\\n• Diurnal variation","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/410aa6199cb2c04127bc.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/a9df5cecbb46a2400bbb.png"}]},{"n":16,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"An infant was brought with failure to thrive. On examination, the infant appears floppy with an enlarged tongue, hypotonia, skeletal muscle weakness, and hepatomegaly. The chest X-ray is shown below. Laboratory investigations reveal elevated levels of serum creatine kinase (CK), aspartate transaminase (AST), alanine transaminase (ALT), lactate dehydrogenase (LDH), and urine glucose tetrasaccharide. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Von Gierke disease","Pompe disease","Mc Ardle disease","Tarui disease"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Pompe disease\\n\\nFrom the above-given clinical scenario and the radiograph showing\xa0cardiomegaly,\xa0Pompe\'s disease\xa0is the most likely diagnosis.\\n\\nPompe\'s disease (Type 2 glycogen storage disease) is an autosomal recessive disorder that occurs due to α-1,4-glucosidase (acid maltase) deficiencyleading to lysosomal glycogen accumulation in cardiac, skeletal, and smooth muscle cells. It can appear within weeks or months after birth (infantile Pompe disease) or may appear late in juvenile, childhood, or adulthood disease (late-onset Pompe disease).\\n\\nThe clinical symptoms of Pompe disease are hypotonia (floppy infant),\xa0skeletal muscle weakness, hepatomegaly, hypertrophic cardiomyopathy,\xa0macroglossia, and chest X-ray image shows cardiomegaly.\\n\\nLaboratory findings will show elevated levels of serum creatine kinase (CK), aspartate transaminase (AST), alanine transaminase (ALT), and lactate dehydrogenase (LDH). Urine glucose tetrasaccharide, a glycogen breakdown metabolite, is a reliable biomarker for disease severity and treatment response.\\n\\nMuscle biopsy shows the presence of vacuoles that stain positively for glycogen.\xa0It is diagnosed by detecting the decreased activity of the enzyme,\xa0α-1,4-glucosidase in the\xa0dried blood spots, leukocytes, blood mononuclear cells, muscle, or cultured skin fibroblasts.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Von Gierke disease\xa0occurs due to a deficiency of glucose-6-phosphatase.\xa0Patients\xa0present commonly in 3-4 months of age with\xa0hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, hepatomegaly, growth retardation, hyperuricemia, and hyperlipidemia.\\n\\nOption C: McArdle disease occurs due to myophosphorylase deficiency leading to reduced ATP generation.\xa0The typical manifestations are myoglobinuria (secondary to rhabdomyolysis), muscle pain and cramps after heavy exercise. This pain may reduce after a brief period of rest and the patient may be able to perform an exercise. This is called the\xa0second wind phenomenon. Cardiomyopathy is not seen in this condition.\\n\\nOption D: Tarui disease\xa0(Type VII glycogen storage disorder)\xa0occurs due to the deficiency of muscle phosphofructokinase. The main clinical features are exercise intolerance, cramps, hemolytic anemia, and myoglobinuria. There is no hypertrophic cardiomyopathy.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/a61ebe475801718fe841.png"]},{"n":17,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following is not associated with G6PD deficiency?\\n\\nGlucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD)","options":["Predisposes the patient to oxidative stress","Patient presents with hemolysis","Defective NAPDH synthesis","Equal incidence in males and females"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Equal incidence in males and females\\n\\nG6PD Deficiency does not have an equal incidence in males and females. It is an X-linked recessive (XLR) disorder, hence males are more affected than females.\\n\\nGlucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency causes congenital hemolytic anemia due to enzyme deficiency.\xa0G6PD is required to generate NADPH while oxidizing glucose-6-phosphate in the hexose monophosphate pathway (HMP). G6PD deficiency leads to reduced NADPH levels (option B) which then reduces the level of reduced glutathione. Reduced glutathione is required to protect the cells against oxidative stress to RBCs and hemolysis.\\n\\nClinically, most patients are asymptomatic. When exposed to triggers like fava beans, infections, drugs such as primaquine, chloroquine, sulfonamides/sulfones, dapsone, and nitrofurantoin patient develops:\\n\\n• Episodic hemolysis, both intravascular and extravascular\\n• Hemolytic anaemia\\n• Hemoglobinemia\\n• Hemoglobinuria\\n\\nIncreased oxidative damage to RBCs leads to the formation of denatured precipitates known as Heinz bodies. These bodies can damage the RBC membrane leading to intravascular hemolysis. Additionally, when such RBCs pass through the splenic cords, macrophages pluck out these precipitates, giving them the characteristic appearance of bite cells.\\n\\nThe images below show the presence of Heinz bodies and bite cells.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/77ac1180a2b0a137602e.jpg"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/559c7c003ccdaa3f4bc7.jpg"}]},{"n":18,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 26-year-old female patient comes with a history of chronic weight loss, loss of appetite, and diarrhea along with pale bulky stools and is diagnosed with malabsorption syndrome. Steatorrhea is defined in this patient by stool fat exceeding:","options":["14 g per day","7 g per day","32 g per day","28 g per day"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. 7 g per day\\n\\nSteatorrhea is a symptom\xa0defined by stool fat exceeding 7 g per day.\\n\\nSteatorrhea is the passage of greasy, foul-smelling, difficult-to-flush stools.\xa0It is often associated with weight loss & nutritional deficiencies due to concomitant malabsorption of amino acids & fat-soluble vitamins. It is one of the clinical features of fat malabsorption and is noted in many conditions causing intraluminal maldigestion, mucosal malabsorption, or lymphatic obstruction.\\n\\nCauses of steatorrhea:\\n\\n•\\n\\nExocrine pancreatic insufficiency: Chronic pancreatitis, cystic fibrosis (CF), pancreatic tumors\\n\\n•\\n\\nBile acid deficiency: Primary biliary cholangitis\xa0(PBC), primary sclerosing cholangitis (PSC), ileal resection, Crohn disease, small\xa0intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO)\\n\\n•\\n\\nEnteropathy or\xa0Malabsorption syndrome: Celiac disease (most common), tropical sprue, giardiasis, Whipple disease, amyloidosis, SIBO, abetalipoproteinemia\\n\\nScreening of steatorrhea is by the detection of fat\xa0by Sudan III staining. Stool fat can be quantified\xa0by calculating the coefficient of fat absorption (CFA), which is an essential step in the diagnosis.\\n\\nThe following are the general tests for malabsorption:\\n\\nDisorder\\nInvestigation\\n\\nFat malabsorption\\n\\nQuantitative: 24h fecal fat >7g\\n\\nQualitative: Sudan III staining\\n\\nC14\xa0Triolein breath test\\n\\nFecal steatocrit\\n\\nCarbohydrate malabsorption\\n\\nD-xylose test\\n\\nLactose hydrogen breath test\\n\\nProtein malabsorption\\nC13\xa0egg-white breath test\\n\\nThe following are the specific tests for malabsorption:\\n\\nDisorder\\nInvestigation\\n\\nCeliac disease\\n\\nAnti-tissue transglutaminase (anti-tTG)\\n\\nAnti-endomysial antibodies\\n\\nMucosal biopsy showing villous atrophy, crypt hyperplasia, lamina propria infiltrates, and normal mucosal thickness.\\n\\nWhipple\'s disease\\nMucosal biopsy showing PAS +ve macrophages in lamina propria\\n\\nPancreatic insufficiency\\n\\nFecal elastase\\n\\nFecal chymotrypsin\\n\\nSmall intestinal bacterial overgrowth (SIBO)\\n\\nDue to Enterobacter or Pseudomonas - jejunal aspirate\xa0>103\xa0CFU\\n\\nDue to other organisms - jejunal aspirate\xa0>105\xa0CFU","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":19,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 20-year-old female patient presents with the following finding as shown in the image below. On further investigation, she is found to have reduced levels of the enzyme UDP-glucuronosyltransferase. Which of the following disorders is she most likely suffering from?","options":["Type I Crigler-Najjar syndrome","Gilbert syndrome","Dubin Johnson syndrome","Rotor syndrome"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Gilbert syndrome\\n\\nWith the given clinical scenario and with reduced levels of enzyme UDP-glucuronosyltransferase, the patient is most likely suffering from Gilbert syndrome.\\nGilbert syndrome is an autosomal recessive condition characterized by decreased activity of the enzyme\xa0UDP-glucuronosyltransferase, which is responsible for the conjugation of bilirubin. This leads to unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia, that presents as yellowish discolouration of the sclera (icterus/jaundice) as seen in the given image.\\n\\nIndividuals with Gilbert syndrome are usually asymptomatic except for intermittent episodes of jaundice, which usually occurs for the first time during adolescence, due to changes in the concentration of sex hormones.\xa0Some patients may have complaints like malaise, fatigue, nausea, anxiety, loss of appetite, and abdominal discomfort. Symptoms are usually precipitated by triggers such as fasting, intercurrent illness, menstruation, and dehydration.\\n\\nAdministration of phenobarbital shows normalisation of the serum bilirubin concentration and hepatic bilirubin clearance.\\n\\nGilbert syndrome usually doesn\'t require any treatment.\\n\\nOther options:\\nOption A: Type I Crigler-Najjar syndrome is characterized by a complete deficiency of the enzyme\xa0UDP-glucuronosyltransferase. It usually presents as severe congenital jaundice (serum bilirubin levels over 20 mg/dL) and accompanying brain damage.\\nOptions C and D: Dubin Johnson and Rotor syndrome\xa0both have\xa0elevated conjugated bilirubin in the blood, and are due to defective secretion of conjugated bilirubin into the bile.","img":false,"explImg":false,"images":["/simg/418c4cb5bf31b9cf2590.jpg"]},{"n":20,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 4-year-old child has a history of biting his lips and fingers. On evaluation, you notice lip lacerations, missing portions of fingers of his hands, and developmental delay. On inquiry, the parents say that two male cousins on the mother\'s side with similar complaints have been diagnosed with a genetic disorder. Lab reports reveal hyperuricemia. Which enzyme is most likely deficient in this child?","options":["Purine nucleoside phosphorylase","Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase","Adenine phosphoribosyltransferase","Adenosine deaminase"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario of self-mutilating behavior, delayed developmental milestones, positive family history and hyperuricemia is highly suggestive of Lesch-Nyhan syndrome. It occurs due to the complete\xa0deficiency of\xa0the enzyme hypoxanthine-guanine phosphoribosyltransferase (HGPRT)\xa0involved in the salvage pathway.\\n\\nPurines produced in the body or introduced through diet can be converted to nucleoside triphosphates to be reused by the body. This pathway is called the salvage pathway for purines and is shown below\\n\\nLesch-Nyhan syndrome is an X-linked recessive disorder due to a complete deficiency of the HGPRT enzyme. This deficiency results in an inability to salvage hypoxanthine or guanine resulting in increased levels of phospho-ribosyl pyrophosphate (PRPP) and decreased levels of inosine monophosphate (IMP) and guanosine monophosphate (GMP). An increase\xa0in guanine and hypoxanthine eventually gets converted into uric acid,\xa0There is\xa0increased de novo purine synthesis.\\n\\nThe clinical features include compulsive self-mutilation,\xa0intellectual disability, delayed milestones, dystonic movements, and hyperuricemia. The excess uric acid can form kidney stones\xa0(urolithiasis) or deposits in joints as urate crystals (gouty arthritis).\\n\\nThe treatment of this condition includes:\\n\\n• Allopurinol\xa0or febuxostat for hyperuricemia\\n• Behavioural management using restraints for self-mutilation\\n• High fluid intake, reduced fructose and purine intake\\n\\nPartial deficiency of HGPRT leads to\xa0Kelley-Seegmiller syndrome.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Purine nucleoside phosphorylase deficiency leads to severe T-cell deficiency with normal B-cell function. Patients present with autoimmune diseases and opportunistic infections.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Adenine phosphoribosyltransferase (APRT)\xa0is another enzyme along with HGPRT to be involved in the salvage of purine bases to nucleotides.\\n\\nOption D:\xa0Adenosine deaminase deficiency leads to sparse or defective T cells and B cells. Patients suffer from severe immunodeficiency and often succumb to fatal infections.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/07224f924b311013ff3e.jpg"}]},{"n":21,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"An undernourished child is brought for consultation. You prescribe a mineral supplement that is absorbed by the mechanism shown in the image below. Identify the supplement.","options":["Zinc","Iron","Copper","Calcium"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Iron\\n\\nThe given image shows iron absorption and transport from a duodenal enterocyte into the blood, hence the mineral supplement prescribed can be identified as an iron supplement.\\n\\nThe following image shows in detail, the absorption of iron from a duodenal enterocyte.\\n\\nDietary iron is available in two forms - heme and non-heme iron. Initially, the ferric non-heme iron is reduced to ferrous iron by duodenal cytochrome B (ferric reductase)\xa0or by vitamin C.\xa0The reduced ferrous iron is then transferred into the enterocyte by DMT 1 (divalent metal transporter).\\n\\nHeme iron moves into the cytoplasm with the help of transporters. Here, it is metabolized to release ferrous iron, which then follows the same pathway as non-heme iron.\\n\\nOnce inside the enterocyte, it can either get stored as ferritin or transferred across the basolateral membrane by ferroportin.\xa0In the plasma, iron is transported in the Fe3+ form bound to transferrin, which is a transport protein. Hephaestin is a ferroxidase that oxidizes Fe2+ to Fe3+ before it is transported.\\n\\nHepcidin, a protein secreted from the liver plays a major role in the regulation of iron absorption. When the body’s iron stores are adequate it downregulates the expression of ferroportin and thereby iron absorption too. Thus, iron is trapped in the intestinal cells which is eventually sloughed off. Hepcidin also inhibits the release of iron from macrophages by binding to ferroportin.\xa0High hepcidin levels are seen in systemic inflammation and low hepcidin levels are seen in hemochromatosis.\\n\\nIron is an important component of\\n\\n• Hemoglobin\\n• Myoglobin\\n• Cytochrome P450 groups of enzymes\\n• Components of the electron transport chain\\n• Ribonucleotide reductase","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/4be464fb546d8bc96e48.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/fc1465f50436f3777920.jpg"}]},{"n":22,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A patient with a history of using well water for cooking and consumption presents with vomiting and abdominal pain. On examination, there is jaundice, hemoglobinuria, Mees lines on the nails, and features of hypovolemic shock. A toxicology screen is done, and the 24-hour urine sample is found to contain high amounts of a toxin. Which enzyme of Kreb\'s cycle will be inhibited by this toxin?","options":["Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase","Alpha-ketoglutarate deydrogenase","Aconitase","Succinate dehydrogenase"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Alpha-ketoglutarate deydrogenase\\n\\nThe given clinical scenario is suggestive of arsenic toxicity. Alpha-ketoglutarate dehydrogenase\xa0(AKG) of Kreb\'s cycle is inhibited by this toxin, arsenite (inorganic arsenic salt).\\n\\nα-Ketoglutarate is a rate-determining intermediate in Kreb\'s cycle. It\xa0has a crucial role in cellular energy metabolism as it acts as a precursor of glutamate and glutamine and promotes protein synthesis.\\n\\nAlpha-ketoglutarate\xa0undergoes oxidative decarboxylation to form\xa0succinyl-CoA and CO2\xa0with the help of the multienzyme complex, AKG dehydrogenase. This enzyme complex requires the cofactors, thiamin diphosphate, lipoate, NAD+, flavin\xa0adenine dinucleotide (FAD), and coenzyme A.\\n\\nArsenite inhibits this decarboxylation reaction and causes the substrate, α-ketoglutarate, to accumulate.\\n\\nArsenic toxicity is either due to inhalation of toxic arsenic gas or chronic consumption of foods cooked in contaminated well water. It\xa0presents with malaise, headache, nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, jaundice, hemoglobinuria, and renal failure. There may be hemorrhagic gastroenteritis, hypovolemic shock, and cardiovascular manifestations such as QT prolongation, polymorphous ventricular tachycardia, and congestive cardiomyopathy. Mees lines\xa0is a common dermatologic manifestation characterized by transverse white striae on the nail.\\n\\nArsenic toxicity is detected by measuring the 24-hour urine excretion of arsenic.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A:\xa0Glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate dehydrogenase\xa0is inhibited by iodoacetate. It is not a part of Kreb\'s cycle.\\n\\nOption C:\xa0Aconitase is inhibited by fluoroacetate.\\n\\nOption D: Succinate dehydrogenase is inhibited by malonate.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/ca7b45cd9539e6b9c337.jpg"}]},{"n":23,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 7-year-old child is brought with a history of impaired vision and severe photophobia. On examination, he has light hair, light skin color, and pale blue eyes. A disorder due to deficiency in the enzyme tyrosinase is suspected. What is the likely diagnosis?","options":["Albinism","Tyrosinemia type 1","Tyrosinemia type 2","Homocystinuria"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Albinism\\n\\nThe clinical scenario along with the deficiency of the\xa0enzyme\xa0tyrosinase\xa0point to the diagnosis of albinism.\\n\\nOculocutaneous albinism\xa0(OCA) is a group of inherited disorders characterized by a reduction or complete lack of melanin pigment in the skin, hair, and eyes leading to hypopigmentation.\xa0It is either due to defective melanosomes or defective protein involved in the formation and transfer of melanin.\\n\\nThere are 7 types of\xa0nonsyndromic albinism (OCA1 to OCA7). OCA type 1 is characterised by reduced or absent tyrosinase activity.\xa0The first step in melanin synthesis involves the oxidation of L-tyrosine to DOPA by the enzyme tyrosinase.\xa0Tyrosinase further acts on DOPA to form dopaquinone, which undergoes a series of reactions to form melanin.\\n\\nClinical features include\xa0white hair,\xa0white skin, and\xa0iris appearing\xa0blue-grey in oblique light. Ocular manifestations include\xa0photophobia, nystagmus, and\xa0decreased visual acuity.\\n\\nOther options-\\n\\nOption B- Tyrosinemia type 1 is caused due to the deficiency of the enzyme fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase. Clinical findings include\xa0cirrhosis, liver failure, rickets, failure to thrive, peripheral neuropathy, and boiled cabbage odour.\\n\\nOption C- Tyrosinemia type 2 is caused due to the deficiency of the enzyme tyrosine transaminase. Clinical findings include palmoplantar keratosis, painful corneal erosions with photophobia, and learning disability.\\n\\nOption D- Homocystinuria is caused due to cystathionine beta-synthase deficiency. Clinical findings include lens dislocation, thrombotic vascular disease, intellectual disability, and osteoporosis.\\n\\nGiven below is an image of a young child with OCA showing white hair, skin, and pale iris:","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/60975f3cc9edfed53a4c.jpg"}]},{"n":24,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"An exclusively breastfed newborn was brought with vomiting and diarrhea that developed since birth. On examination, the baby is jaundiced and has hepatomegaly. Blood glucose level was low and erythrocyte galactose-1-phosphate level was elevated. What is the diagnosis?","options":["Fructosuria","von Gierke\'s disease","Classical galactosemia","Hereditary fructose intolerance"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. Classical galactosemia\\n\\nThe clinical scenario of an exclusively breastfed infant with vomiting, diarrhea, jaundice, hypotonia, and hepatomegaly with elevated erythrocyte galactose-1-phosphate level is suggestive of classical galactosemia.\\n\\nClassical galactosemia\xa0is an\xa0autosomal recessive disorder.\xa0The enzyme deficient here is\xa0galactose-1-phosphate uridyl transferase (GALT).\xa0Normally, newborns receive up to 40% of their calorie intake as lactose (glucose + galactose). Without GALT, the infant cannot metabolize galactose 1-phosphate,\xa0which\xa0accumulates and causes injury to the kidney, liver, and brain.\\n\\nClinical features include vomiting, hypoglycemia, hepatomegaly, jaundice, congenital cataracts, aminoaciduria, and\xa0failure to thrive. There is an increased incidence of\xa0Escherichia coli neonatal sepsis. Long-term complications include speech delays, learning impairment, motor dysfunction, and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism (in females).\\n\\nThe presence of galactose in urine (positive Benedict\'s test), elevated blood galactose level, and elevated\xa0erythrocyte galactose-1-phosphate level help in confirming the diagnosis.\\n\\nTreatment is a galactose-free diet. Supportive therapy and the prevention of complications are important.\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\nOption A: Fructosuria is due to the deficiency of fructokinase. There are no symptoms except for the benign excretion of fructose in urine.\\n\\nOption B: von Gierke\'s disease is due to\xa0glucose-6-phosphatase\xa0or translocase deficiency. It\xa0is a\xa0glycogen storage disorder (Type I).\xa0 They may\xa0develop hypoglycemia and lactic acidosis in the neonatal period, but hepatomegaly usually develops at 3–4 months. They may\xa0have doll-like facies, short stature, and a protuberant abdomen. Hyperuricemia, the elevation of triglycerides, low-density lipoproteins, and phospholipids are seen.\\n\\nOption D: Hereditary fructose intolerance is due to a deficiency of fructose 1, 6-bisphosphate aldolase (aldolase B). Patients are healthy and symptom-free until fructose or sucrose is ingested. Clinical manifestations may include jaundice, hepatomegaly, vomiting, lethargy, irritability, and convulsions. It will not be seen in newborns who are exclusively on breast milk.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/832add6efa639686128f.jpg"}]},{"n":25,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"You are studying the visual pigment cycle in the physiology lab. Identify the substance that converts cis-retinal to trans-retinal.","options":["Scotopsin","Rhodopsin","Metarhodopsin II","Lumirhodopsin"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Rhodopsin\\n\\nThe substance that converts\xa0cis-retinal to trans-retinal is rhodopsin.\\n\\nRhodopsin\xa0is the visual pigment in the outer segment of\xa0rods responsible for scotopic vision (night/ dark vision). It is a light-sensitive pigment and consists of opsin and 11-cis retinal.\\n\\nWhen light is absorbed by rhodopsin, it decomposes very quickly. This leads to an instantaneous change of the cis form of retinal into an all-trans form.\xa0This all-trans-retinal separates from scotopsin to produce bathorhodopsin. Bathorhodopsin is very unstable and decays to form lumirhodopsin, which then decays and forms metarhodopsin I, metarhodopsin II, and finally, into the completely split products scotopsin and all-trans-retinal.\\n\\nMetarhodopsin II is also called activated rhodopsin, and\xa0it produces excitatory changes in the rods, which transmits optic nerve action potentials. to the central nervous system.\\n\\nRe-formation of rhodopsin involves the conversion of the all-trans-retinal back into 11-cis retinal. This requires metabolic energy and is catalyzed by retinal isomerase. 11-cis retinal then recombines with scotopsin to form rhodopsin. Rhodopsin now remains stable until it is triggered by light energy to start decomposing again.\\n\\nRole of vitamin A: The second route of re-formation of rhodopsin involves the conversion of the all-trans-retinal\xa0into all-trans-retinol, which is a\xa0form of vitamin A. All-trans-retinol is converted into 11- cis retinol by isomerase. 11-cis retinol is then converted into 11-cis retinal, combines with scotopsin, and forms new rhodopsin.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/9e55e29e7ca2f45c0e81.jpg"}]},{"n":26,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following modifications occur posttranscriptionally to produce a mature eukaryotic mRNA?\\n\\n1. Capping\\n2. Polyadenylation\\n3. Phosphorylation\\n4. Trimming\\n5. Splicing\\n6. Hydroxylation","options":["1, 2, 4","3, 5, 6","1, 2, 5","3, 4, 6"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Correct answer: C. 1, 2, 5\\n\\nCapping,\xa0polyadenylation, and\xa0splicing\xa0are the modifications that occur posttranscriptionally to produce a mature eukaryotic mRNA.\xa0Phosphorylation,\xa0hydroxylation, and\xa0trimming\xa0are modifications that occur\xa0posttranslationally.\\n\\nA mature eukaryotic mRNA is formed after the pre-mRNA components of the heterogenous nuclear RNA (hnRNA) undergo the following posttranscriptional modifications in the nucleus:\\n\\n1. Addition of a 5′ cap: A 7-methylguanosine is attached to the 5′ terminal end of the mRNA via a 5′-to-5′ triphosphate linkage. This stabilizes the mRNA and allows initiation of translation.\\n2. Addition of a 3′-poly-A tail: The poly-A tail is added by the nuclear enzyme, polyadenylate polymerase, after clevage of the polyadenylation signal sequence (AAUAAA) found near the 3′ end of the mRNA. This terminates eukaryotic transcription. Furthermore, it stabilizes the mRNA, allows it to exit from the nucleus, and helps in translation.\\n3. Splicing: This is the process of removing introns (intervening sequences that do not code for proteins) and joining exons (the coding or expressed sequences), carried out by the spliceosome. Alternative splicing occurs in different tissues to produce varied number of proteins from a limited set of genes.\\n\\nProtein coding RNA\\n\\nNon Protein coding RNA\\n\\nmRNA\\n\\nRibosomal RNAs (rRNA)\\n\\nLong noncoding RNAs (lncRNAs)\\n\\nTransfer RNAs (tRNA)\\n\\nSmall nuclear RNAs (snRNAs)\\n\\nMicro and silencing RNAs (miRNAs and siRNAs)","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":27,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"A 15-month-old child is brought because of developmental delay and clouded appearance of the cornea in both eyes. The other examination findings of the child are shown in the image below. The elder sibling of the child also has similar findings. What is the most likely diagnosis?","options":["Mucopolysaccharidoses","Von Gierke’s disease","Down’s syndrome","Refsum disease"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Correct answer: A. Mucopolysaccharidoses\\n\\nThe given image shows coarse facial features, umbilical hernia, claw hand, and thoracolumbar gibbus. These features along with corneal opacity and developmental delay are highly suggestive of mucopolysaccharidoses (MPS).\\n\\nThe features mentioned in the above question are suggestive of MPS type 1. It is caused by the deficiency of α-l-iduronidase, leading to the storage of dermatan and heparan sulfate. The classical form of MPS 1 is known as Hurler syndrome, the intermediate form as Hurler–Scheie, and the most attenuated form is known as Scheie syndrome.\\n\\nMucopolysaccharidoses (MPS) are a group of genetic disorders (inborn errors of metabolism) affecting glycosaminoglycans (GAGs). They are inherited in an autosomal recessive manner and involve mutations of genes encoding lysosomal hydroxylases.\xa0Diagnosis is by demonstration of GAGs in urine or tissue biopsy samples, assays of suspected defective enzymes in white blood cells, fibroblasts, or serum, and testing for specific genes.\\n\\nThe given image shows cloudy cornea in mucopolysaccharidosis:\\n\\nOther options -\\n\\nOption B: Von Gierke’s disease or glycogen storage disease type 1a - Occurs due to the deficiency of glucose-6-phosphatase, and affected children present with hepatosplenomegaly and fasting hypoglycemia.\\n\\nOption C: Down\'s syndrome\xa0occurs due to trisomy 21. Affected children present with a flattened facial profile,\xa0oblique palpebral fissures, and epicanthic folds. Patients may be intellectually disabled.\\n\\nOption D: Refsum disease\xa0- It is a disorder of fatty acid metabolism. It occurs due to deficiency of alpha-hydroxylase causing phytanic acid accumulation in the tissues. It presents with neurological symptoms, cerebellar ataxia, retinitis pigmentosa, and polyneuropathy.\\n\\nDisease\\nMPS\\nEnzyme defective\\nGAGs affected\\nSymptoms\\n\\nHurler-, Scheie-,\\nHurler-scheie syndrome\\nMPS I\\nα-L-Iduronidase\\n\\nDermatan sulfate,\\nHeparan sulfate\\n\\nMental retardation,\\ncoarse facial features,\\nHepatosplenomegaly\\nCloudy cornea\\n\\nHunter syndrome\\nMPS II\\nIduronate sulfatase\\nDermatan sulfate,\\nHeparan sulfate\\nMental retardation\\n\\nSanfilippo\xa0syndrome A\\nMPS IIIA\\nHeparan sulfate-N-sulfatase\\nHeparan sulfate\\nDelay in development\\nMotor dysfunction\\n\\nSanfilippo\xa0syndrome B\\nMPS III B\\nα-N-Acetylglucosaminidase\\nHeparan sulfate\\nAs MPS III A\\n\\nSanfilippo\xa0syndrome C\\nMPS III C\\nα-glucosaminide N-acetyltransferase\\nHeparan sulfate\\nAs MPS III A\\n\\nSanfilippo\xa0syndrome D\\nMPS III D\\nN-acetylglucosamine 6-sulfatase\\nHeparan sulfate\\nAs MPS III A\\n\\nMorquio syndrome A\\nMPS IV A\\nGalactosamine\xa06-sulfatase\\nKeratan sulfate\\nChondroitin 6-sulfate\\nSkeletal dysplasia\\nshort stature\\n\\nMorquio syndrome B\\nMPS IV B\\nβ-galactosidase\\nKeratan sulfate\\nAs MPS IV A\\n\\nMaroteaux-lamy\xa0syndrome\\nMPS VI\\nN-acetylgalactosamine 4-sulfatase\\nDermatan sulfate\\nCurative of spine,\\nShort stature\\nSkeletal dysplasia\\nCardiac defects\\n\\nSly\xa0syndrome\\nMPS VII\\nβ-glucuronidase\\nDermatan sulfate\\nHeparan sulfate\\nChondroitin 4-sulfate\\nChondroitin 6-sulfate\\n\\nShort stature\\nSkeletal dysplasia\\nHepatomegaly\\nCloudy cornea\\n\\nNatowicz\xa0syndrome\\nMPS IX\\nHyaluronidase\\nHyaluronic acid\\nJoint Pain\\nShort stature","img":false,"explImg":true,"images":["/simg/bfc6ea5d850a6c6d573c.jpg"],"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/14f7e7aaf3e3b218e458.png"}]},{"n":28,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"Which of the following blot transfer techniques is used to detect specific sequences of DNA?","options":["Northern blot","Western blot","Southwestern blot","Southern blot"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. Southern blot\\n\\nSouthern blot transfer technique can be used to detect specific sequences of DNA. It involves the following steps.\\n\\n• DNA from a cell line or tissue is digested into fragments by restriction enzymes\\n• The fragments are embedded in agarose or polyacrylamide gel and subjected to electrophoresis. This separates the fragments based on their charge and size\\n• The electrophoresed fragments are then denatured with a mild alkali and transferred to nitrocellulose or nylon paper\\n• A labelled DNA probe is added, which hybridizes with complementary strands of DNA\\n• The hybridisation is visualised by X-raying the film or using an image screen\\n\\nOther options:\\n\\n• Western blot is used to detect and quantify specific protein molecules\\n• Northern blot is used to detect and quantify specific RNA molecules\\n• South-western blot or overlay blot is used to examine protein-protein or protein-nucleic acid interactions","img":false,"explImg":false},{"n":29,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"What is the correct sequence of events in Southern blotting?\\n\\n1 - Transfer to a membrane\\n2 - Hybridization with a labeled probe sequence\\n3 - Separation of DNA fragments by electrophoresis","options":["2, 1, 3","2, 3, 1","1, 2, 3","3, 1, 2"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Correct answer: D. 3, 1, 2\\n\\nIn Southern blotting DNA fragments are first separated by electrophoresis.\\n\\n• The DNA in the gel is then made single-stranded by exposure to dilute alkali, and transferred by capillary action to the nylon membrane, which is laid over it. The relative positions of the different DNA fragments are thus preserved on the membrane.\\n• The membrane is then soaked in buffer containing the labelled probe, which base pairs with the DNA fragment of interest.\\n• The position of that fragment is then determined by autoradiographic (exposure of the membrane to X-ray film), or fluorescent detection of the hybridized probe.","img":false,"explImg":true,"explBlocks":[{"t":"text"},{"t":"img","src":"/simg/0143495d732377d56cae.jpg"}]},{"n":30,"subject":"Biochemistry","text":"You are evaluating the metabolic changes in patients with various conditions. Increased basal metabolic rate will be seen in patients with which of the following condition?","options":["Protein energy malnutrition","Acromegaly","Starvation","Addison disease"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Correct answer: B. Acromegaly\\n\\nIncreased basal metabolic rate (BMR) is seen in patients with acromegaly.\\n\\nHigh energy-requiring states such as fever, pregnancy, pheochromocytoma, adrenergic agonist drugs, cancer, congestive heart failure, acromegaly, polycythemia, and Paget’s disease of the bone are associated with high BMR. Obesity, starvation or anorexia, hypogonadism, Addison\'s syndrome, and immobilization are associated with low BMR.\\n\\nBMR is the energy required by an awake individual during rest. It is the minimum energy required to carry out vital functions of the body such as respiration, circulation, etc.\\n\\nIt is calculated using oxygen consumption, calorific value (energy produced by 1 liter of oxygen), and surface area.\\n\\nBMR is dependent on various factors:\\n\\n• Men have more BMR than women of the same body weight and age because women’s bodies contain proportionally more adipose tissue and fewer muscles.\\n• Elderly people have less BMR\xa0as the muscle tissue gets infiltrated with fat as age advances.\\n• Low environmental temperature is associated with high BMI.\\n• Exercise increases BMR.\\n• The thyroid hormone has a direct role in increasing BMR.","img":false,"explImg":false}]}')}}]);